# CASP15 T1152 — The Cell Wall Sensor (7R1L)
---

How do organisms detect bacteria?
One answer is by sensing their cell walls.

This is a LysM domain from Clostridium thermocellum —
a tiny protein module that recognizes the building
blocks of bacterial cell walls.

> close
> load 7r1l
---

At just 56 residues, this is one of the smallest
CASP15 targets. But don't let the size fool you.

LysM domains are found across all kingdoms of life —
in bacteria, fungi, plants, and animals —
and they all do the same thing:
bind chitin and peptidoglycan.

> spin 0.15
---

This structure shows the LysM domain
bound to its carbohydrate target.

The cooperative binding is key — multiple LysM
domains work together to grip the long sugar
chains in bacterial cell walls, creating
a much stronger interaction than any
single domain could achieve.

> stop
> view ligand
---

In plants, LysM domains are the first line
of immune defense — they detect bacterial
cell wall fragments and trigger
immune responses.

In biotechnology, they're used to break
down biomass for biofuel production.

> reset
> spin 0.1
---

The LysM domain — small but mighty,
nature's universal cell wall detector.
PDB code 7R1L, CASP15 target T1152.

> stop
